        ['A sheet lists item prices in B2:B10 and the VAT rate in E1. Write the formula in C2 to calculate VAT and explain why it needs an absolute reference when copied down.', ['Correct formula', 'Relative B2', 'Absolute E1', 'Explanation'], 'C2: =B2*$E$1. When copied down to C3, C4 and so on, B2 changes to B3, B4 for each item, but $E$1 stays pointing to the VAT rate. Without the dollar signs it would change to E2, E3, which are empty.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 5],
        ['Explain three common spreadsheet error values and how to fix each.', ['Three errors', 'Cause', 'Fix'], '#DIV/0! means division by zero or a blank cell; check the divisor. #NAME? means an unrecognised function or name; check spelling. #REF! means a reference to a deleted cell; correct the formula. ##### means the column is too narrow; widen it.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Describe how to use conditional formatting to highlight marks below 50 in red.', ['Select range', 'Conditional Formatting', 'Less than 50', 'Red format'], 'Select the range of marks. Click Home, then Conditional Formatting, then Highlight Cells Rules, then Less Than. Type 50 and choose a red fill or text format. Click OK.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 4],
        ['Explain how data validation can reduce errors when entering pupils\' marks.', ['Restriction', 'Example rule', 'Error message', 'Fewer errors'], 'Data validation restricts entries to whole numbers from 0 to 100, so impossible values such as 150 or text are rejected with an error message. An input message can remind the user of the rule.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 4],
